    {
      "crop_slug": "raspberry",
      "field_path": "cane_management_seasoned",
      "our_prose": "Cane management is the heart of growing raspberries, because the crown is perennial but each cane is biennial: it grows one year, fruits the next, then dies. How you prune depends entirely on the type. On SUMMER-BEARING (floricane-fruiting) raspberries, first-year primocanes grow vegetatively, overwinter, and fruit the following early summer as floricanes, then die; immediately after harvest cut those spent floricanes to the ground and remove them, and in late winter thin the surviving primocanes to 4 to 6 sturdy canes per foot of row, narrow the row to about a foot, and tie the canes to a trellis. On EVERBEARING (primocane-fruiting, or fall-bearing) raspberries, the first-year canes fruit on their tips in late summer or fall; the simplest and recommended management is to mow every cane to the ground in late winter for a single, clean, heavy fall crop with no training of overwintered canes and reduced disease and cane-borer pressure. The same everbearing planting can instead be double-cropped, leaving the lower canes to fruit again as floricanes the next summer, but the single-crop mow is far easier and the usual home choice. Red and yellow raspberries also sucker aggressively from the roots and need their spread contained to the row every season.",
      "run_normalized": "primocane fruiting or fall bearing raspberries",
      "run_in_our_prose": "primocane-fruiting, or fall-bearing) raspberries",
