      "field_path": "type_selection_seasoned",
      "our_prose": "The first choice is cane type, and it sets everything else. Summer-bearing (floricane-fruiting) raspberries fruit once, in early summer, on canes grown the previous year; you prune by removing the spent floricanes and thinning the new primocanes. Everbearing (primocane-fruiting, also called fall-bearing) types fruit on first-year canes in late summer or fall, and can simply be mowed to the ground each winter for one clean crop and the easiest pruning. Beyond that, red and yellow raspberries sucker and are the hardiest; black and purple raspberries do not sucker, are less hardy and more disease-prone, but are richly flavored. In the hot South, only heat-tolerant low-chill types succeed.",
